.task-board/EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-jnpbyz_packet-bridge-hev-spike/TASK-260715-2jatnd_record-m0-packet-bridge-decision/README.md:7:In scope: public-API compliance; correctness; selected MTU, socket buffers, batch budgets, and session baseline; error and drop behavior; physical-device memory headroom; known gaps; upstream pin; notice status; fork decision; revalidation triggers; downstream M1 readiness. Out of scope: SSH or relay gate claims, production route and DNS approval, future performance guarantees, and authorizing a fork without Instruments evidence.
.task-board/EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-jnpbyz_packet-bridge-hev-spike/TASK-260715-2jatnd_record-m0-packet-bridge-decision/README.md:10:1. A TASK-ID-scoped ADR links every required M0 Bridge row to reproducible evidence and marks pass, fail, or blocked without averaging away a red platform or address-family result. 2. The record selects initial MTU, requested socket buffers, batch count and time budgets, HEV settings, and measured session ceiling with rationale. 3. Pass requires public API only, correct IPv4 and IPv6 framing, bounded backpressure, clean lifecycle, no nominal unexplained loss, and physical-device memory headroom. 4. A fork is rejected by default and allowed only when Instruments identifies bridge copies or syscalls as a material bottleneck and a measured callback prototype improves it with regression coverage and rebase plan. 5. Residual risks, device and OS revalidation triggers, notices, and tasks unblocked for M1 are explicit.
.task-board/EPIC-260715-21g2pi_product-experience-and-security/STORY-260715-309t4z_connection-control-and-status-ui/TASK-260715-39lo79_add-connection-presentation-and-control-model-tests/README.md:7:In scope: every NETunnelProviderSession state, current/stale/missing/mismatched snapshots, full/degraded/reasserting/failed predicates, reasons, connect/disconnect/retry, permission/configuration errors, duplicate commands, out-of-order callbacks, app recreation, profile switch/edit/delete, policy changes, version skew, cancellation, observer/resource cleanup, and property/table tests. Out of scope: UI pixels, packet forwarding correctness already owned by M1-M3, physical system indicator, and release signing.
.task-board/EPIC-260715-21g2pi_product-experience-and-security/STORY-260715-309t4z_connection-control-and-status-ui/TASK-260715-39lo79_add-connection-presentation-and-control-model-tests/README.md:10:1. Table tests cover every contract input row and assert presentation state, label/value, capability, reason, primary/secondary actions, and accessibility announcement. 2. Full is impossible without all required current-generation capabilities; degraded requires TCP plus safe DNS; stale/missing data never synthesizes connected capability. 3. Command/race tests cover double tap, stop during start, start during stop, permission denial, late success, timeout, app recreation, profile switch rollback, and policy edit conflicts. 4. Repeated observer/snapshot/command/model recreation cycles return tasks, notifications, timers, managers, and retained closures to baseline. 5. Tests use controllable clocks/events with no sleeps and publish exact commands and redacted result evidence.
.task-board/EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-2xnj3v_apple-capability-provisioning/README.md:1:# Gate P0 — Apple capabilities and physical-device provisioning
.task-board/EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-2xnj3v_apple-capability-provisioning/README.md:4:Prove that the Relux Works organization can provision the required host and packet-tunnel extension identities and can install and launch a minimally functional custom VPN provider on a physical iPhone and an Apple-silicon Mac. The result is a reproducible signing and entitlement baseline for later M0 integration, not a production VPN.
.task-board/EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-2xnj3v_apple-capability-provisioning/README.md:7:In scope: organization enrollment and required roles; bundle ID, App Group, and Keychain access-group naming; Network Extension packet-tunnel capabilities; development certificates and profiles; disposable iOS and macOS host/provider probes; NETunnelProviderManager configuration; entitlement and embedded-profile inspection; physical-device install, launch, start, and stop evidence. Out of scope: TestFlight or App Store release, Developer ID notarization, production UI, packet forwarding, SSH transport, relay behavior, and storage of certificates, private keys, credentials, or provisioning secrets in the repository or board.
.task-board/EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-2xnj3v_apple-capability-provisioning/README.md:10:1. Evidence confirms Relux Works organization enrollment, team identifier, responsible roles, registered physical devices, and access to manage identifiers, capabilities, certificates, and profiles. 2. A reviewed identity matrix defines unique iOS and macOS host and extension bundle IDs plus the minimum App Group and Keychain groups. 3. Host and extension App IDs and development profiles contain the exact required packet-tunnel, App Group, and Keychain entitlements with no unrelated capability. 4. A disposable provider is installed through its containing app, saves a NETunnelProviderManager configuration, launches, reports a versioned probe result, and stops cleanly on one named physical iPhone and one named Apple-silicon Mac. 5. Exported entitlement and profile inspections match the planned matrix and contain no secret material. 6. A Gate P0 record includes device and OS versions, Xcode and source revisions, bundle/profile identifiers, commands or steps, observed results, failures, and an explicit pass or fail verdict.
