.task-board/EPIC-260716-3fyjn0_manual-validation-and-approvals/STORY-260716-2e86nb_m3-physical-validation/TASK-260715-2wnw59_run-mac-ipv6-sleep-captive-matrix/README.md:7:In scope: named Apple-silicon Mac and supported macOS or toolchain; Wi-Fi and Ethernet where available; IPv4, IPv6, dual-stack, and controlled NAT64 or IPv6-only fixture when available; compatible and fail-closed modes; full and degraded capability; sleep and wake; captive fixture; host-app kill; cancel during start; stop reasons; repeated loops; TCP, DNS, UDP, and QUIC probes; authorized captures; memory, energy, routes, counters, cleanup. Out of scope: unsupported Intel claims, unauthorized public networks, production traffic, forcing unavailable cellular behavior, absolute kill-switch claims, or waiving red rows.
.task-board/EPIC-260716-3fyjn0_manual-validation-and-approvals/STORY-260716-2e86nb_m3-physical-validation/TASK-260715-2wnw59_run-mac-ipv6-sleep-captive-matrix/README.md:10:1. A TASK-ID-scoped evidence bundle records hardware, macOS and toolchain, revisions, profile, interface and family, route mode, fixture, event timeline, traffic, routes, snapshots, counters, footprint, energy, captures, and raw references for every row. 2. Supported address-family rows use the actual endpoint and exact current exclusion and keep ordinary DNS and application traffic on the exit path or fail explicitly. 3. Interface, sleep or wake, captive, and mode transitions restore current full or degraded capability without recursive routes, stale associations, physical ordinary fallback, or deadlock. 4. Host-app termination does not stop a healthy provider, while cancellation, stop reasons, and repeated loops return routes, sessions, channels, tasks, timers, sockets, and descriptors to baseline. 5. NAT64, interface, API, or captive rows unavailable on the test hardware are recorded unavailable rather than passed, and any macOS behavior change carries reproduction evidence and an owner.
.task-board/EPIC-260715-2qzczm_resilience-and-performance/STORY-260715-1zzt0c_windows-rekey-and-memory-controls/README.md:7:In scope: control, ordinary, bulk, and relay window profiles; capped bandwidth-delay-product calculation; window-credit accounting; WINDOW_ADJUST withholding; byte and elapsed-time rekey; server-initiated rekey; key-exchange admission pauses; lane-local rekey failure; advisory os_proc_available_memory sampling; soft, pressure, and critical watermarks; HEV sessions and caches, socket buffers, DNS cache, relay buffers, channels, lane count, queued bytes, and reconnect overlap in one ledger; fast refusal and controlled failure; privacy-safe metrics; fault, allocation-bound, soak, and physical-memory tests. Out of scope: selecting the SSH engine, claiming advertised credit is eagerly allocated, relying on jetsam as control flow, changing relay framing, final performance constants before measurement, live flow migration, or application UI.
.task-board/EPIC-260715-2qzczm_resilience-and-performance/STORY-260715-1zzt0c_windows-rekey-and-memory-controls/README.md:10:1. Initial windows are configurable per channel class, use 32 KiB and 64 KiB candidates plus a capped bulk BDP calculation, and cannot multiply an uncapped default by maximum sessions. 2. Window adjustment is bounded by the current ledger and is withheld under pressure without attempting to revoke prior credit. 3. Client byte and time thresholds and server requests survive active direct-tcpip and relay traffic, while new-channel assignment pauses safely during key exchange. 4. Rekey failure isolates the affected lane first, emits a finite reason, and cannot leak, duplicate, or migrate existing byte streams. 5. The memory controller measures all declared consumers, treats available memory as uncached advisory input, and applies the specified soft, pressure, and critical actions in order. 6. Old and replacement transports cannot coexist beyond an explicit reservation, and critical pressure releases the old transport before replacement or stops explicitly. 7. Fault and allocation tests plus a physical at-least-5-GiB mixed soak record footprint, peak, available memory, sessions, windows, queues, channels, drops, rekeys, descriptors, and cleanup with no unbounded growth or avoidable jetsam.
.task-board/EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-lkshfz_ssh-engine-spike/TASK-260715-2d3g5e_add-common-ssh-transport-conformance-tests/README.md:7:In scope: fake-clock and deterministic socket or server fixtures; host-key first use, match, change, and rejection; Ed25519 and fallback auth; direct-tcpip and exec; concurrent channels; bounded write pressure; EOF, half-close, reset, and early close; window profiles; client byte and time rekey; server rekey; keepalive; cancellation at each state; metrics and descriptor baselines. Out of scope: candidate-specific pass exceptions, production lane scheduling, full physical performance matrix, relay framing, UI, and tests that rely only on sleeps.
.task-board/EPIC-260715-21g2pi_product-experience-and-security/STORY-260715-n5dt84_onboarding-accessibility-and-migration/README.md:7:In scope: first-run and resumable onboarding, SSH host prerequisite guidance, privacy acknowledgement before VPN enablement, profile/trust/permission handoffs, empty and recoverable error states, settings/help entry points, VoiceOver and macOS accessibility, keyboard navigation, focus order, Dynamic Type and text scaling, contrast, reduced motion, localization-ready copy, pseudo-localization, shared accessibility identifiers, current SOCKS coexistence/replacement/retirement decision, approved defaults/history migration, iOS/macOS UI tests, and physical end-to-end acceptance. Out of scope: implementing a public SOCKS server, OpenSSH config import, ProxyJump, release notarization, TestFlight upload, App Store submission, regional licensing decisions, and unsupported locale translations.
.task-board/EPIC-260715-21g2pi_product-experience-and-security/STORY-260715-n5dt84_onboarding-accessibility-and-migration/README.md:10:1. A first-time user on iOS and macOS can understand the self-hosted SSH model, complete or resume prerequisites, create a profile, trust a host, acknowledge privacy terms, grant VPN permission, connect, understand capability or failure, obtain help, and disconnect. 2. Privacy disclosure appears before the first enable action and onboarding does not claim anonymity, a Relux exit network, or capabilities not present in the selected mode. 3. All M4 surfaces have stable shared accessibility identifiers, meaningful labels/hints/values, logical focus, keyboard operation on macOS, VoiceOver operation, supported text scaling, sufficient contrast, and reduced-motion behavior. 4. User-facing strings are externalized, pseudo-localization and text expansion pass, and the approved launch-locale policy and fallback are documented. 5. A reviewed decision selects legacy SOCKS coexistence, replacement, or retirement and defines handling of host/account/port defaults, existing release history, bundle/storage identity, rollback, and user messaging before migration code runs. 6. Automated UI journeys plus named physical iPhone and Apple-silicon Mac acceptance cover first run, relaunch/resume, permission denial, empty/error recovery, migration path, full/degraded/failure states, diagnostics, accessibility, and screenshot review.
