.task-board/EPIC-260716-3fyjn0_manual-validation-and-approvals/STORY-260716-2cjf4i_m2-physical-validation/TASK-260715-10phgg_run-macos-full-degraded-recovery-validation/README.md:7:In scope: supported physical Apple-silicon Mac and macOS; stable Ethernet or Wi-Fi starting path; controlled authenticated SSH host; supported and unsupported relay fixture; full startup; install or handshake failure; relay process or lane loss; controlled TCP flow; general UDP probes; UDP and TCP DNS clients; safe-DNS failure; reprobe recovery; provider stop; physical and exit captures; snapshots; memory and counters; redacted evidence. Out of scope: path transitions, sleep or wake, NAT64 transition, captive recovery, includeAllNetworks, final QUIC policy, notarization, production traffic, legacy app behavior, and unsupported Intel client hardware.
.task-board/EPIC-260716-3fyjn0_manual-validation-and-approvals/STORY-260716-2cjf4i_m2-physical-validation/TASK-260715-10phgg_run-macos-full-degraded-recovery-validation/README.md:10:1. Evidence records Mac, macOS and Xcode, source and dependency revisions, profile generation, relay asset and protocol identity, reason fixture, resolver and exit fixture, network type, timestamps, duration, snapshots, memory, queues, associations, drops, and authorized captures. 2. Full startup advertises UDP only after relay validation; forced relay-unavailable startup advertises degraded only after controlled TCP and safe DNS work. 3. Runtime relay loss invalidates general UDP, keeps eligible TCP and safe DNS, reports the exact reason, and physical capture shows zero ordinary UDP or DNS fallback; safe-DNS loss moves to failed. 4. Relay repair and reprobe restore full only after a fresh identity and handshake, and subsequent IPv4 or IPv6 UDP succeeds through new exit-host associations without old-generation delivery. 5. Repeated full, degraded, failed, restore, and stop rows stay within the memory budget, clean routes and resources, and attach a TASK-ID-scoped redacted reproducible evidence bundle.
.task-board/EPIC-260715-2qzczm_resilience-and-performance/STORY-260715-19ii11_performance-and-resource-tuning/TASK-260715-2kchi0_record-performance-measurement-protocol/README.md:7:In scope: named iPhone and Mac baselines; OS, toolchain, source and dependency revisions; profile and server config; MTU, HEV, lane, window, rekey, QUIC, route, reconnect, and memory parameters; web-like, bulk, idle, DNS, UDP burst, mixed, and concurrency workloads; loss and latency; lifecycle and failure rows; warmup, steady state, repetitions, outliers, confidence or practical-effect rule; throughput, TTFB, DNS latency, RTT, CPU, energy, memory, packet and syscall rate, queues, drops, cleanup; raw evidence manifest and redaction. Out of scope: running benchmarks, choosing results in advance, public-user traffic, unmeasured tuning, marketing claims, or hiding red rows.
.task-board/EPIC-260715-2qzczm_resilience-and-performance/STORY-260715-19ii11_performance-and-resource-tuning/TASK-260715-2kchi0_record-performance-measurement-protocol/README.md:10:1. A TASK-ID-scoped protocol defines exact workload generators, payload integrity checks, impairment profiles, concurrency stages, durations, warmup, cooldown, repetitions, random seeds, and environment controls for every metric family. 2. Every row records device, OS, toolchain, revisions, server, algorithms, profile, route and QUIC policy, all parameter values, traffic shape, counters, timestamps, raw artifact references, and privacy classification. 3. Statistical and practical-effect rules define baseline comparison, noise handling, sample exclusion, regression, material improvement, and pass or red outcomes without post-hoc threshold changes. 4. Safety gates for byte correctness, zero ordinary DNS fallback, route loops, bounded queues, memory ceilings, failure behavior, and cleanup cannot be traded away for throughput or energy. 5. The protocol defines how unavailable device or network rows, failed runs, Instruments traces, capture authorization, redaction, and accepted configuration or regression updates are preserved and reviewed.
.task-board/EPIC-260716-3fyjn0_manual-validation-and-approvals/STORY-260716-2cjf4i_m2-physical-validation/TASK-260715-2m7lwo_run-macos-udp-and-dns-exit-validation/README.md:1:# Verify Mac IPv4, IPv6, UDP, DNS, and exit-host behavior
.task-board/EPIC-260716-3fyjn0_manual-validation-and-approvals/STORY-260716-2cjf4i_m2-physical-validation/TASK-260715-2m7lwo_run-macos-udp-and-dns-exit-validation/README.md:4:Run the M2 full-mode acceptance matrix on a named physical Mac, proving representative IPv4 and IPv6 UDP plus tunnel-owned DNS exit through the selected SSH host with bounded resources, no ordinary physical fallback, and repeatable cleanup.
.task-board/EPIC-260716-3fyjn0_manual-validation-and-approvals/STORY-260716-2cjf4i_m2-physical-validation/TASK-260715-2m7lwo_run-macos-udp-and-dns-exit-validation/README.md:7:In scope: supported physical Apple-silicon Mac and macOS; generated development provider; controlled SSH exit; controlled IPv4 and IPv6 UDP echo or application fixtures; domain-destination fixture; approved resolver; dig or equivalent UDP and TCP DNS clients; authorized physical and exit captures; stable Ethernet or Wi-Fi starting path; association and drop counters; memory; relay process evidence; repeated start and stop; redaction. Out of scope: network transitions, sleep or wake, NAT64 transition, captive recovery, degraded-mode transitions, final QUIC policy, notarization, production traffic, legacy product behavior, and unsupported Intel client hardware.
.task-board/EPIC-260716-3fyjn0_manual-validation-and-approvals/STORY-260716-2cjf4i_m2-physical-validation/TASK-260715-2m7lwo_run-macos-udp-and-dns-exit-validation/README.md:10:1. Evidence records Mac, macOS and Xcode, source and dependency revisions, relay asset and protocol identity, profile generation, network type, resolver and exit fixtures, limits, MTU, timestamps, duration, memory, associations, queues, drops, and authorized capture points. 2. Representative IPv4 and IPv6 UDP request and response payload hashes match through the selected SSH host and exit capture confirms the relay host opened destination sockets. 3. Domain-destination UDP and tunnel-owned DNS succeed through relay UDP, TC or forced fallback uses SSH DNS-over-TCP, and physical-interface capture observes zero ordinary queries to local resolvers. 4. Oversize, association limit, queue pressure, relay process loss, and provider stop produce bounded declared outcomes with no application UDP sent directly on the physical interface. 5. Repeated runs return provider, HEV, adapter, channel, association, remote socket, timer, buffer, route, and DNS state to baseline and attach a TASK-ID-scoped redacted evidence bundle.
.task-board/EPIC-260716-3fyjn0_manual-validation-and-approvals/STORY-260716-2cjf4i_m2-physical-validation/TASK-260715-10phgg_run-macos-full-degraded-recovery-validation/progress.md:24:- [ ] Run full startup degraded startup runtime failure DNS failure restore and stop on Mac
.task-board/EPIC-260716-3fyjn0_manual-validation-and-approvals/STORY-260716-2cjf4i_m2-physical-validation/TASK-260715-10phgg_run-macos-full-degraded-recovery-validation/progress.md:25:- [ ] Capture truthful snapshots traffic behavior and zero ordinary physical fallback
.task-board/EPIC-260715-2qzczm_resilience-and-performance/STORY-260715-3ao1u9_nat64-sleep-and-captive-validation/README.md:4:Harden and validate the M3 runtime across IPv6-only NAT64, address-family changes, sleep and wake, captive-network transitions, containing-app termination, provider stops, and repeated lifecycle loops on named physical iPhone and Mac configurations.
.task-board/EPIC-260715-2qzczm_resilience-and-performance/STORY-260715-3ao1u9_nat64-sleep-and-captive-validation/README.md:7:In scope: NAT64 synthesized endpoint capture and exclusion replacement; IPv4, IPv6, dual-stack, and IPv6-only transitions; sleep and wake event coalescing; path and reconnect ownership; captive negotiation within Apple system exceptions; compatible and fail-closed behavior; safe DNS continuity or explicit failure; UI process termination independence; cancellation during start; provider stop reasons; repeated start and stop; stale-event rejection; deterministic simulation; authorized physical captures; device, OS, revision, configuration, memory, route, counter, and cleanup evidence; support-matrix regression triggers. Out of scope: private Network Extension APIs, promising captive traffic is tunnelable when Apple excludes it, physical DNS fallback, automatic host-key replacement, final marketing claims, unsupported hardware, or tuning without the performance story evidence process.
.task-board/EPIC-260715-2qzczm_resilience-and-performance/STORY-260715-3ao1u9_nat64-sleep-and-captive-validation/README.md:10:1. A versioned device, OS, interface, address-family, route-mode, event, traffic, and capture matrix names every required row, fixture, metric, expected outcome, and evidence location before physical execution. 2. IPv6-only and NAT64 starts and transitions use the actual connected endpoint and an exact current-generation exclusion, preserve IPv4 and IPv6 application behavior where supported, and never deadlock in stale routes. 3. Sleep and wake produce at most one current reconnect sequence, cancel stale work, restore only truthful capability, and keep reconnect overlap within the memory ceiling. 4. Captive-network handling matches compatible and fail-closed disclosures and Apple exceptions while ordinary traffic and DNS remain tunneled or fail explicitly. 5. Containing-app termination does not tear down a healthy provider, while cancellation, provider stop, and repeated lifecycle loops release routes, tasks, timers, channels, associations, sockets, and descriptors. 6. Deterministic fault tests cover synthesized-address changes, sleep races, captive transitions, UI termination, stop during start, late callbacks, and API capability changes. 7. Physical iPhone and Mac evidence records every supported row, identifies red or unavailable rows without waiver, and publishes regression triggers when OS behavior differs.
.task-board/EPIC-260716-3fyjn0_manual-validation-and-approvals/STORY-260716-2mtjdn_m4-product-decisions-and-validation/README.md:4:M4 human work: physical product/security/accessibility matrices (VoiceOver, system approvals) and product decisions - privacy/support copy approval, legacy SOCKS coexistence decision, launch locales and copy ownership.
.task-board/EPIC-260715-2qzczm_resilience-and-performance/STORY-260715-19ii11_performance-and-resource-tuning/TASK-260715-yjpk5a_implement-approved-minimal-hev-fork/README.md:4:Only if the Instruments gate explicitly approves a fork, implement the smallest upstream-trackable HEV callback-ingress change, preserve all packet and backpressure contracts, add regression coverage, and demonstrate the required physical before-and-after improvement. If the gate rejects a fork, close this task without code and link the decision.
.task-board/EPIC-260715-2qzczm_resilience-and-performance/STORY-260715-19ii11_performance-and-resource-tuning/TASK-260715-yjpk5a_implement-approved-minimal-hev-fork/README.md:7:In scope after positive gate only: exact approved upstream commit; minimal callback ingress and egress seam; preserved IPv4 and IPv6 family semantics; bounded ownership and lifetime; EAGAIN or ENOBUFS, EMSGSIZE, cancellation, and cleanup behavior; existing socket-pair fallback or rollback as approved; licenses and notices; patch documentation; rebase strategy; unit, fuzz, integration, physical, and performance comparison. Out of scope: work before gate approval, private APIs, utun descriptor discovery, unrelated HEV changes, protocol or SOCKS behavior changes, speculative tuning, or accepting speed without correctness and memory gates.
.task-board/EPIC-260715-2qzczm_resilience-and-performance/STORY-260715-19ii11_performance-and-resource-tuning/TASK-260715-yjpk5a_implement-approved-minimal-hev-fork/README.md:10:1. Work begins only with a positive TASK-260715-3mnqn8 decision naming the exact callback contract, upstream commit, expected material improvement, and required gates; a negative decision closes this task with no source change. 2. The patch is limited to the approved seam, retains upstream attribution and required notices, and documents patch series, ABI, ownership, lifetime, rollback, and rebase procedure. 3. IPv4 and IPv6 packet boundaries, family handling, MTU limits, bounded drops, batching budgets, cancellation, stop, and malformed-frame behavior match or strengthen the unmodified baseline. 4. Unit, fuzz, HEV integration, lifecycle, memory, and physical regressions pass with no task, descriptor, buffer, session, or footprint growth. 5. Named-device before-and-after evidence meets the approved practical improvement threshold for representative workloads and preserves route, DNS, latency, energy, and safety gates or the fork is rejected and rolled back.
.task-board/EPIC-260716-3fyjn0_manual-validation-and-approvals/STORY-260716-2cjf4i_m2-physical-validation/TASK-260715-2m7lwo_run-macos-udp-and-dns-exit-validation/progress.md:23:- [ ] Run representative IPv4 IPv6 domain UDP and DNS rows on a named Mac
