## 2026-07-21

### 0823 — Baseline DNS uses explicit exit-side numeric resolvers (TASK-260715-1tnjlu)
- DECISION: ADR-022 proposes one required ordered per-profile `dnsResolver` with one to four canonical numeric IPv4/IPv6 `dns53` endpoints and per-endpoint port default 53. There is no product/public-vendor default, hostname endpoint, physical resolver inheritance, exit-host shell discovery, DoH, or fake-DNS baseline. M1 uses reusable DNS-over-TCP through authenticated SSH; M2 reuses the same endpoint identity for relay UDP with TCP fallback.
- SAFETY: Resolver validation and SSH `direct-tcpip` readiness precede route/DNS settings. Post-settings ordinary queries have only the virtual ingress -> SSH -> explicit endpoint path. Endpoint exhaustion returns bounded SERVFAIL, withdraws safe-DNS capability, and clears settings; it cannot call the device resolver. Missing legacy resolver data yields `requiresResolverConfiguration` with no inferred migration.
- SCHEMA/PLANNING: Runtime configuration advances to schema 2 when `dnsResolver` lands; TASK-260721-33o8fc owns the schema/codec/migration implementation and TASK-260721-2raag7 owns the focused cross-platform profile experience. Existing M1 upstream, routing, M2 relay/degraded, documentation, harness, and physical leak tasks are linked or listed in the decision impact map.
- EVIDENCE: `.research/260721_task-260715-1tnjlu-exit-dns-resolver-policy.md` compares all four required policies and cites RFC 1035/7766/8484/4035/5952 plus accepted project contracts. `diagrams/TASK-260715-1tnjlu_dns-policy-flow.dot` renders and was visually inspected; ADR-022 remains proposed until independent reviewer acceptance.

### 0736 — TCP admission is atomic, bounded, and destination-free (TASK-260715-zfg9ap)
- DECISION: `TCPAdmissionRegistry` is one generation-scoped mutex authority for pending handshakes, authenticated flows, concurrent channel opens, owned channels, fixed directional queue capacity, live buffered bytes, and phase counts. All ceilings and the measured-safe/HEV evidence gates are caller-injected; denial is synchronous, retains no waiter, maps to SOCKS5 `REP=01`, and explicitly prohibits an SSH open. Reservation identifiers are monotonically allocated without reuse and fail closed with finite `identifier_capacity` pressure before `UInt64` wrap.
- OWNERSHIP: Handshake, flow, and opening tokens provide once-only release plus deinit rollback. Flow termination atomically releases an in-flight open, owned channel, reserved/live bytes, and phase slot before publishing one finite terminal reason. A late open completion after terminal cleanup is counted as a discarded event rather than a second release. Existing admitted flows continue byte/buffer progress while new work is rejected under pressure.
- DIAGNOSTICS: Runtime schema v1 is additively extended to 139 fixed counters, 75 fixed gauges, and two 12-bucket cumulative histograms. Registry-owned current gauges publish through a generation-scoped fixed-size atomic coalescer and reconcile into snapshots independently of the bounded event lane, so dropped counter/histogram ingestion remains observable without leaving phantom flow/open/channel/byte gauges. The TCP surface contains only aggregate pressure/terminal tokens, counts, bytes, health, and channel-open latency; it accepts no destination, hostname, address, port, payload, credential, flow/channel ID, or free-form label. Maximum populated encoding is 13,568 bytes of the 65,536-byte limit and generation advancement isolates stale publications.
- VERIFICATION: Eighteen admission tests cover below/at/above every independent ceiling, 128/256-way reserve races, 500 release/open and 500 late-callback races, 250 explicit-release/deinit ownership races, ID exhaustion, queued-byte overflow, session-health races, 8,000 sustained pressure rejects alongside 2,000 admitted-flow progress steps, every terminal reason across 320 churn lifecycles, and exact local/exported baseline recovery. The bounded-queue convergence probe passes 20/20 runs; focused normal and TSan admission/diagnostics suites pass; `make validate-core` passes boundary/native gates, 306 tests in 27 suites, and the post-test build; strict recursive Swift format, privacy/boundary, diff, and board validation pass.

### 0710 — Bounded full-duplex pump owns fixed reservations and independent terminal handoff (TASK-260715-sdnk2k)
- DECISION: `ReluxTunnelCore` now owns a candidate-neutral local nonblocking byte-stream/readiness seam and one structured full-duplex pump. Each direction retains exactly one current `Data` plus offset; no next read occurs until that buffer drains. Caller-injected chunk/write/fairness/no-progress limits are validated, and a shared non-waiting reservation actor caps aggregate pump-owned bytes across concurrent flows without a capacity queue.
- CANCELLATION: One locked control signal wakes local readiness, cancels both structured child tasks, calls `SSHByteChannel.cancel()` once, discards late positive completions, joins both directions, and releases the aggregate reservation once. EOF, cancellation, local/remote closure, read/write error, zero progress, and bound violation are handed to the later lifecycle owner as finite directional events; the pump does not choose half-close/reset policy.
- COVERAGE: Swift Testing exercises exact bidirectional hashes (`6d440a59…f5ad`, `66f02f2a…4378`), randomized fragmentation/partial writes/alternating local pressure, twelve changing seeds, SSH-credit suspension, fairness progress, all five suspending seams, late completion, typed failures, aggregate denial, no-spin permanent pressure, and a 100-run cleanup baseline.
- VERIFICATION: Focused normal and Thread Sanitizer suites pass 12 tests, twenty repeated seeded runs pass, strict recursive Swift format passes, and the second `make validate-core` passes 288 tests in 26 suites plus build. The first full run exposed one unrelated existing concurrent provider-failure assertion (`iOS` expected 1007, observed 1009); its isolated rerun and the full rerun passed without pump changes.

### 0638 — Private HEV SOCKS pending admission rejects before protocol bytes (TASK-260715-1juybj)
- CORRECTION: The normative ownership sequence now matches `HEVSOCKSBoundary.swift:284-299`: immediately after TCP accept/socket setup, an already-stopped or generation-retired boundary or a full pending-authentication set closes the descriptor without a SOCKS5 method reply, RFC 1929 reply, authentication enqueue, or adapter handoff.
- BOUNDARY: Wrong/stale capability and the M1 monotonic accept-to-auth deadline remain authentication-phase failures with only phase-and-remaining-budget-safe replies. The post-handoff adapter resource reservation is separate from the boundary pending-authentication ceiling.
- VERIFICATION: Both PlantUML sources pass syntax/render checks; the corrected 1921 x 1742 opaque ownership render was inspected at original resolution with the admission branches and all labels legible/unclipped. The focused external no-auth rejection test passes.

### 0558 — Private HEV SOCKS admission and TCP byte ownership are frozen (TASK-260715-1juybj)
- DECISION: The shared iOS/macOS HEV boundary remains IPv4 loopback plus a fresh per-generation RFC 1929 capability; loopback/TCP peer metadata is explicitly not process identity. A descriptor reaches `SSHProxyAdapter` only after capability proof and is positioned at the first CONNECT-request byte.
- CORRECTION: Current `HEVSOCKSBoundary` uses `SO_RCVTIMEO`, which is a per-receive inactivity bound and can restart as bytes arrive; authentication sends have no deadline. The pending set is count-bounded, but current source does not prove an absolute accept-to-auth deadline. M1 must compute one monotonic deadline at accept, apply its remaining budget across greeting/auth reads, comparisons, and replies without progress extension, and prove slow-trickle/reply-stall eviction plus slot/descriptor release on iOS and macOS.
- CONTRACT: IPv4, IPv6, and strict ASCII/IDNA domain CONNECT requests map once to the accepted candidate-neutral `SSHTransport.openDirectTCPIP` seam, using the verbatim remote-resolved destination, constant sanitized `127.0.0.1:0` originator, caller-injected `SSHChannelPolicy`, and no retry, lane migration, or optimistic success. Each direction owns one bounded buffer; EOF halves remain independent; first-terminal-claim cleanup releases socket, channel, tasks, timers, and reservations once.
- RESOURCE: Adapter capacity is an atomic non-waiting reservation bounded by the HEV session ceiling and the measured M0/SSH/Core memory equation; exact flow, chunk, queue, window, and timeout values remain injected measurement inputs for M1/M3 rather than promoted constants. Diagnostics are aggregate schema-v1 only and exclude credentials, SOCKS bytes, endpoints, hostnames, payloads, and flow identifiers.
- TRACEABILITY: The accepted-input resource names nonexistent `TASK-260715-100wu6`; the accepted SSH seam is `TASK-260720-100wu6`. This is recorded as an input-ID typo only, with no SSH engine/observability decision reopened.

