 ## 2026-07-21
 
 ### 0823 — Baseline DNS uses explicit exit-side numeric resolvers (TASK-260715-1tnjlu)
-- DECISION: ADR-022 proposes one required ordered per-profile `dnsResolver` with one to four canonical numeric IPv4/IPv6 `dns53` endpoints and per-endpoint port default 53. There is no product/public-vendor default, hostname endpoint, physical resolver inheritance, exit-host shell discovery, DoH, or fake-DNS baseline. M1 uses reusable DNS-over-TCP through authenticated SSH; M2 reuses the same endpoint identity for relay UDP with TCP fallback.
-- SAFETY: Resolver validation and SSH `direct-tcpip` readiness precede route/DNS settings. Post-settings ordinary queries have only the virtual ingress -> SSH -> explicit endpoint path. Endpoint exhaustion returns bounded SERVFAIL, withdraws safe-DNS capability, and clears settings; it cannot call the device resolver. Missing legacy resolver data yields `requiresResolverConfiguration` with no inferred migration.
-- SCHEMA/PLANNING: Runtime configuration advances to schema 2 when `dnsResolver` lands; TASK-260721-33o8fc owns the schema/codec/migration implementation and TASK-260721-2raag7 owns the focused cross-platform profile experience. Existing M1 upstream, routing, M2 relay/degraded, documentation, harness, and physical leak tasks are linked or listed in the decision impact map.
-- EVIDENCE: `.research/260721_task-260715-1tnjlu-exit-dns-resolver-policy.md` compares all four required policies and cites RFC 1035/7766/8484/4035/5952 plus accepted project contracts. `diagrams/TASK-260715-1tnjlu_dns-policy-flow.dot` renders and was visually inspected; ADR-022 remains proposed until independent reviewer acceptance.
+- DECISION: ADR-022 proposes one required ordered per-profile `dnsResolver` with a non-empty list of canonical numeric IPv4/IPv6 `dns53` endpoints and per-endpoint port default 53. There is no product/public-vendor default, hostname endpoint, physical resolver inheritance, exit-host shell discovery, DoH, or fake-DNS baseline. The earlier one-to-four endpoint ceiling and 5/5/15/10-second, 32/128-query values had no accepted evidence and are removed. New TASK-260721-3miqh4 owns measured endpoint/message/byte/capacity/timing defaults and hard caps; schema, transport, relay, and M4 consumers are blocked on its independently accepted `DNSRuntimePolicyV1`.
+- FAILURE OWNERSHIP: One runtime generation owns one active endpoint and one reusable TCP connection epoch. EOF, timeout, framing/correlation, partial-write ambiguity, channel, or SSH failure atomically retires the epoch, classifies all in-flight queries, and permits one coordinator to replay only eligible QUERY operations as one ordered batch at the next endpoint. Cancelled/expired/terminal work does not retry; late old-epoch callbacks cannot answer; promotion clears cache/transport generation. A valid response including any RCODE never shops resolvers.
+- M2 BOUNDARY: An eligible client-UDP query gets at most one relay UDP attempt at the active endpoint. Only TC=1, pre-response size inability, UDP timeout, or typed relay failure permits M2 to request same-endpoint TCP; M1 then owns TCP reuse and later endpoint promotion. Structural work is one UDP plus at most one TCP attempt per configured endpoint under one injected deadline. Client TCP goes directly to M1; malformed/mismatched UDP does not select another resolver.
+- SAFETY/PLANNING: Resolver/policy validation and SSH `direct-tcpip` readiness precede route/DNS settings. Post-settings ordinary queries have only the virtual ingress -> authenticated SSH/relay -> explicit endpoint path. Endpoint exhaustion returns bounded SERVFAIL, withdraws safe-DNS capability, and clears settings; it cannot call the device resolver. Missing legacy resolver data yields `requiresResolverConfiguration` with no inferred migration. TASK-260721-33o8fc owns schema/codec/migration, TASK-260721-2raag7 owns M4 profile experience, and revised TASK-260715-5o6jqg/TASK-260715-28jdml/TASK-260715-336ljl own shared-failure, M2, and integration evidence.
+- EVIDENCE: `.research/260721_task-260715-1tnjlu-exit-dns-resolver-policy.md` compares all four policies and cites RFC 1035/7766/8484/4035/5952 plus RFC 4254 exit-side `direct-tcpip` semantics and accepted project contracts. `diagrams/TASK-260715-1tnjlu_dns-policy-flow.dot` renders and was visually inspected after the coordinated retry/M2 update. ADR-022 remains proposed until independent reviewer acceptance; no human vendor/product decision remains.
 
 ### 0736 — TCP admission is atomic, bounded, and destination-free (TASK-260715-zfg9ap)
 - DECISION: `TCPAdmissionRegistry` is one generation-scoped mutex authority for pending handshakes, authenticated flows, concurrent channel opens, owned channels, fixed directional queue capacity, live buffered bytes, and phase counts. All ceilings and the measured-safe/HEV evidence gates are caller-injected; denial is synchronous, retains no waiter, maps to SOCKS5 `REP=01`, and explicitly prohibits an SSH open. Reservation identifiers are monotonically allocated without reuse and fail closed with finite `identifier_capacity` pressure before `UInt64` wrap.
