 ## 2026-07-21
 
 ### 0823 — Baseline DNS uses explicit exit-side numeric resolvers (TASK-260715-1tnjlu)
-- DECISION: ADR-022 proposes one required ordered per-profile `dnsResolver` with one to four canonical numeric IPv4/IPv6 `dns53` endpoints and per-endpoint port default 53. There is no product/public-vendor default, hostname endpoint, physical resolver inheritance, exit-host shell discovery, DoH, or fake-DNS baseline. M1 uses reusable DNS-over-TCP through authenticated SSH; M2 reuses the same endpoint identity for relay UDP with TCP fallback.
-- SAFETY: Resolver validation and SSH `direct-tcpip` readiness precede route/DNS settings. Post-settings ordinary queries have only the virtual ingress -> SSH -> explicit endpoint path. Endpoint exhaustion returns bounded SERVFAIL, withdraws safe-DNS capability, and clears settings; it cannot call the device resolver. Missing legacy resolver data yields `requiresResolverConfiguration` with no inferred migration.
-- SCHEMA/PLANNING: Runtime configuration advances to schema 2 when `dnsResolver` lands; TASK-260721-33o8fc owns the schema/codec/migration implementation and TASK-260721-2raag7 owns the focused cross-platform profile experience. Existing M1 upstream, routing, M2 relay/degraded, documentation, harness, and physical leak tasks are linked or listed in the decision impact map.
-- EVIDENCE: `.research/260721_task-260715-1tnjlu-exit-dns-resolver-policy.md` compares all four required policies and cites RFC 1035/7766/8484/4035/5952 plus accepted project contracts. `diagrams/TASK-260715-1tnjlu_dns-policy-flow.dot` renders and was visually inspected; ADR-022 remains proposed until independent reviewer acceptance.
